          <a href="#quotes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Getting a Fair Quote</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Sealing a deck yourself typically costs roughly $0.30 to $0.80 per square foot in materials, or about $100 to $250 for a 300-square-foot deck, while hiring a pro typically runs roughly $1.50 to $3.50 per square foot including basic cleaning, or about $450 to $1,050 for the same deck. Heavy prep such as stripping an old finish or sanding can add roughly $1 to $3 per square foot to a pro quote. Those are typical US ranges, and they vary with region, deck condition, the product you choose and how many railings and stairs are involved. Prices also change from year to year, so treat every figure here as a planning estimate rather than a quote. This guide breaks down coverage math so you can figure out how many gallons you need, shows typical costs for decks from 200 to 500 square feet, lists the add-ons that inflate quotes, and compares what different sealer types cost over ten years, which is where the cheap option often stops being cheap.
        </p>

        <div id="by-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Much Does It Cost to Seal a Deck by Size?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck size is the starting point for any estimate because both materials and labor scale with square footage. To find yours, multiply length by width for each rectangular section and add them together. Stairs and railings are extra surface area that many people forget, and on some decks they add a surprising amount of both material and time. The table below covers the deck floor only, with notes on railings after it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Deck size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer needed (one coat)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">DIY materials (typical)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pro clean and seal (typical)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">200 sq ft (10 x 20)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 to 1.5 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $60 to $160</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $300 to $700</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">300 sq ft (12 x 25)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.5 to 2 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $90 to $240</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $450 to $1,050</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">400 sq ft (16 x 25)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 to 3 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $120 to $320</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $600 to $1,400</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">500 sq ft (20 x 25)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2.5 to 3.5 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $150 to $400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $750 to $1,750</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The DIY column covers sealer plus a deck cleaner and basic supplies, assuming you already own a brush or a pump sprayer. If you need to buy or rent equipment, add that on top, which we cover below. The pro column assumes a deck in fair condition that needs a cleaning but not stripping or sanding. A deck with peeling stain, heavy graying or damaged boards will cost more to prepare.
          </p>

          <p>
            Railings and stairs are the most common reason a quote comes in higher than the square-foot math suggests. Balusters have four sides each and take far longer to coat than flat boards. Some contractors price railings by the linear foot, others fold them into a higher per-square-foot rate. If your deck has long runs of railing, ask how they are priced, and expect them to add noticeably to both cost and sealer use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Very small decks often cost more per square foot when hired out because contractors have minimum job charges to cover travel and setup. A 100-square-foot landing may cost close to the same as a 200-square-foot deck. Large decks tend to come in at the lower end of the per-square-foot range because setup is spread across more area. Multi-level decks with lots of stairs and cuts land at the higher end.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Sealer Do You Need, and What Does It Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer prices vary widely by type. Clear water repellents are the least expensive, typically somewhere around $20 to $40 per gallon at big-box stores. Penetrating oil sealers, toners and semi-transparent sealer-stains typically cost more, commonly around $35 to $70 per gallon, with premium brands at the higher end. Five-gallon pails usually cost less per gallon, which makes sense for larger decks or if you plan to recoat soon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage is the number that matters for the math. Most deck sealer labels list somewhere between roughly 150 and 300 square feet per gallon for one coat, depending on the product and the wood. Rough, weathered and very dry wood absorbs more and sits at the low end. Smooth new boards and dense woods sit at the high end. Some products call for two coats, which roughly doubles the material.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">COVERAGE MATH</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Square feet divided by coverage, plus 10 to 15 percent.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 300-square-foot deck with a sealer rated at 200 square feet per gallon needs 1.5 gallons for one coat. Add railings and stairs, then about 10 to 15 percent extra for absorption and waste, and buy 2 gallons.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Here is an example. A 300-square-foot deck floor plus about 60 square feet of stairs and railing caps comes to 360 square feet. With a sealer rated at 200 square feet per gallon on weathered wood, that is 1.8 gallons for one coat. Add a margin and you are buying 2 gallons. At $25 per gallon for a clear repellent, that is about $50. At $55 per gallon for a premium penetrating oil, it is about $110.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not forget the other materials. A deck cleaner typically costs roughly $15 to $40 for enough to do an average deck, and a wood brightener, if needed, about the same again. Brushes, a roller or pad, a paint tray, plastic sheeting for plants and siding, gloves and painter's tape can add another $30 to $80 if you are starting from scratch. A garden pump sprayer is a useful, inexpensive tool for applying many sealers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pro-pricing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Do Professionals Charge to Seal a Deck?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most deck contractors and painters price sealing by the square foot, and a typical range for a standard clean and seal is roughly $1.50 to $3.50 per square foot. That usually includes washing the deck with a cleaner, letting it dry and applying one coat of sealer. Some contractors include the sealer in the price, while others charge for materials separately. Make sure you know which one you are comparing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Region makes a big difference. Labor costs in high-cost metro areas can push quotes well above the typical range, while rural areas and lower-cost regions often come in below it. Seasonal demand matters too. Spring is the busiest time for deck work in most of the country, and you may find better availability, and sometimes better prices, by booking in late summer or early fall.
          </p>

          <p>
            The product the contractor uses affects the price as well. A clean and clear water repellent sits at the low end. A premium penetrating oil or semi-transparent sealer-stain, which takes more care to apply evenly, sits higher. If you have a particular product in mind, ask whether the contractor will use it and how that changes the quote. Some contractors prefer specific brands they know well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for the difference between sealing and staining in quotes. Many contractors use the words loosely, and a quote for staining may include more prep and a pricier product. If you specifically want a clear or lightly tinted sealer, say so. Our separate guide on <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-stain-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how much it costs to stain a deck</a> covers pricing for pigmented finishes, which usually run higher.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Prep and Add-Ons Raise the Price?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prep is where the real cost difference between decks shows up. A deck that has been maintained on schedule may only need a wash and a day of drying before it is ready. A deck that has gone several years without attention may need a cleaner, a brightener, stripping of the old finish, sanding of raised fibers and replacement of a few boards. Each of those adds time and cost.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Add-on</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">When you need it</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical pro cost (rough)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cleaning with deck cleaner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Almost always</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually included in clean-and-seal pricing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pressure washing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heavy dirt or mildew</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often included; sometimes priced separately</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Brightener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gray wood, after stripping</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often a small add-on per square foot</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stripping old finish</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Peeling or incompatible finish</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $1 to $2 per sq ft extra</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sanding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Raised grain, splinters, rough boards</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $1 to $3 per sq ft extra</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Board replacement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rotted, split or loose boards</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Priced per board, varies widely</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Stripping is the big one. If your deck has an old film-forming stain or paint that is peeling, a penetrating sealer will not soak through it. The old finish has to come off first, usually with a chemical stripper and a scrub or pressure wash, followed by a brightener to neutralize the stripper. That can easily double the cost of the job, which is the best argument for staying on a maintenance schedule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure washing deserves a note. It is often included in a clean-and-seal quote, and it is fast, but done carelessly it can gouge soft wood and raise fibers that then need sanding. Our guide on <a href="/can-you-pressure-wash-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether you can pressure wash a deck</a> explains the safe way. If you are hiring, ask what pressure and tip the contractor uses and whether they follow up with a brightener.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small repairs are worth doing at the same time. Replacing a split board, resetting popped nails or driving in a few deck screws before sealing is far easier than doing it afterward, and it means the new wood gets sealed along with the rest. Contractors typically price board replacement per board or by time and materials, so ask for those prices separately so you can compare quotes fairly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ten-year" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Does It Cost to Keep a Deck Sealed for 10 Years?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The price per gallon is only part of the story. A cheaper sealer that needs reapplying every year can end up costing more over a decade than a pricier one that lasts two or three years, especially once you count cleaner, labor and your own weekends. Comparing the cost per year of protection, rather than the cost per can, gives a fairer picture.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer type (300 sq ft deck)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical interval</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coats in 10 years</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">DIY 10-year materials (rough)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hired 10-year cost (rough)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clear water repellent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About every year</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 10</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $600 to $900</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $4,500 to $8,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Penetrating oil sealer or toner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About every 2 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 5</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $500 to $800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $2,500 to $5,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Semi-transparent sealer-stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About every 2 to 3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $450 to $750</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $2,000 to $4,500</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The table is an illustration, not a forecast. It assumes roughly 2 gallons per coat plus cleaner, typical per-gallon prices for each type and typical hired clean-and-seal rates, and it ignores price changes over time. Your numbers will differ. The pattern, though, is consistent: on a sunny deck, the product with the shortest interval tends to cost the most over a decade, mainly because you are paying for labor, yours or a contractor's, far more often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Labor is the hidden multiplier. For DIY, the difference in materials between product types is modest over ten years, but the difference in weekends spent cleaning and sealing is not. Ten sealing jobs versus four is a lot of Saturdays. For hired work, labor is most of the bill, so the number of times you hire someone matters far more than which product they use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keeping up with maintenance also lowers the cost per job. A deck resealed on time usually only needs cleaning and one coat. A deck that is allowed to fail completely may need stripping, brightening and sanding before it can be resealed, which can double that job's cost. Our guide on <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how often to seal a deck</a> explains the schedules and the water test that tells you when to act.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-vs-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is It Worth Sealing a Deck Yourself?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most homeowners with a simple, ground-level deck in fair condition, DIY sealing is very manageable and saves a lot of money. The work is not technically difficult: clean, rinse, let dry, apply a thin coat, back-brush. A 300-square-foot deck typically takes one day for cleaning and another for sealing, with a drying day in between. The savings over hiring out are often several hundred dollars per job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget for equipment if you do not have it. A pressure washer rental typically costs roughly $50 to $100 per day, though many decks can be cleaned well with a deck cleaner, a stiff brush and a garden hose. A pump sprayer, a deck stain brush or pad on a pole, and a roller for the boards are inexpensive. If you plan to seal every year or two, owning the basic tools pays off quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hiring makes more sense in a few situations. Elevated or second-story decks with lots of railing and stairs are harder and riskier to do yourself. Decks that need stripping and sanding are a lot more work than a simple recoat, and the chemicals involved need careful handling. And if you lack the time or physical ability to spend two weekend days on your knees, a professional's price is a fair trade.
          </p>

          <p>
            A hybrid approach is also common. Some people hire out the heavy prep, stripping and pressure washing, and then apply the sealer themselves. Others hire a pro for the first full restoration and handle the easier maintenance coats after that. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-does-deck-sealer-take-to-dry/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long deck sealer takes to dry</a> helps you plan a DIY schedule around weather, and our roundup of the <a href="/best-deck-sealant/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best deck sealants</a> covers the products we would buy for the job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="quotes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Get a Fair Quote for Deck Sealing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Get at least three quotes, and make sure each one covers the same scope. Write down your deck's square footage, the length of railings, the number of stairs and the current condition before you call. Ask each contractor to break out the cost of cleaning, any stripping or sanding, the sealer itself and labor. That makes it much easier to see where one quote is higher than another.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask which product they will use, how many coats, and whether the price includes materials. A low quote that uses a basic clear repellent is not directly comparable to a higher one using a premium penetrating oil or semi-transparent sealer-stain. Ask how long they expect the finish to last on your deck, and whether they offer any warranty on the work. Get everything in writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check that the contractor understands prep and weather. A good contractor will ask about the current finish, may do a water test, and should talk about letting the deck dry after washing and avoiding rain in the forecast. Be cautious of anyone who plans to wash and seal on the same day, which almost always means sealing damp wood and a shorter-lived finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, remember that prices change. Sealer prices, labor rates and contractor availability shift from year to year and season to season. The ranges in this guide are typical US figures for planning. Your local quotes are the real number. If a quote is far outside these ranges in either direction, ask why. There may be a good reason, such as extra prep, a difficult site or a premium product, or it may be a sign to keep shopping.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying sealer by the square footage alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Labels list coverage for smooth wood, and weathered decks absorb much more. Buying exactly what the math says often leaves you short halfway through, which causes lap marks when you restart. Add railings and stairs to your total, then buy 10 to 15 percent extra. Unopened cans can often be returned.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing quotes with different scopes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                One contractor may include stripping, brightening and a premium sealer while another quotes a quick wash and a clear repellent. The cheaper number is not the better deal if it covers less. Ask every contractor to break out prep, product, coats and labor so you are comparing the same job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the cheapest can every time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A low-cost clear repellent that lasts one year can cost more over a decade than a pricier sealer that lasts two or three, once you count cleaner, labor and your weekends. Compare the cost per year of protection, not the price per gallon, especially on a sunny deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the deck go too long</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Skipping maintenance until the finish fails completely turns a simple clean-and-seal into stripping, brightening and sanding. That can double the cost of the job. Run the water test every spring and reseal when the traffic lanes start absorbing water, while a light maintenance coat is still enough.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting equipment and extras</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A DIY budget that only counts the sealer is incomplete. Deck cleaner, brightener, brushes, rollers, pump sprayers, plastic sheeting and a pressure washer rental can add $50 to $200. List everything before you start so you can compare the real DIY cost with a professional quote fairly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a gallon of deck sealer cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most deck sealer labels list roughly 150 to 300 square feet per gallon for one coat. Rough, weathered or very dry wood absorbs more and lands at the low end, while smooth new boards land at the high end. Check your product's label, and add 10 to 15 percent extra for railings, stairs and absorption.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it cheaper to seal or stain a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sealing is usually a little cheaper per job because clear and lightly tinted sealers often cost less per gallon and need less careful application than pigmented stains. Over time, though, stains often last longer between coats, which can make them cheaper per year of protection. Compare the cost per year rather than the cost per job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to seal a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical 300-square-foot deck, plan on about one day for cleaning and rinsing, at least a day of drying, and another day for sealing. The sealer itself needs about a day before foot traffic and longer before furniture. Prep-heavy jobs involving stripping or sanding can stretch the project across several more days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do deck contractors charge extra for railings?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. Railings and balusters take much longer to coat than flat boards, so some contractors price them by the linear foot while others fold them into a higher square-foot rate. If your deck has long runs of railing, ask how they are priced and make sure every quote includes them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal a deck in one day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rarely, if you do it properly. Cleaning a deck soaks the wood, and most sealer labels call for a dry surface, usually after 24 to 48 hours of dry weather. Washing and sealing on the same day often traps moisture, which shortens the life of the finish. Plan on at least two days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing a deck add value to a home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sealing alone does not add much measurable value, but it protects the value of the deck itself and makes it look cared for, which helps when selling. A weathered, gray deck with splinters can be a negative in a listing. A deck in good condition is part of overall curb appeal and saves buyers from repair costs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the cheapest way to seal a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Doing it yourself with a clear water repellent, a deck cleaner and a pump sprayer is the cheapest per job, often under $100 for a small deck. Over several years, a longer-lasting penetrating sealer can be cheaper per year because you apply it less often. Keeping up with maintenance avoids expensive stripping and sanding later.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

